                <opener><seg rendition="#center">No. 241.</seg></opener>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Foulk to Mr. Bayard</hi>.</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Legation of the United States</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Seoul, Corea</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1885-07-05"><hi rendition="#italic">July</hi> 5, 1885</date>.
                        (Received August 15.)</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 193.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to report that a Corean
                    gentleman, who had been instructed as to methods of searching for coal-beds by
                    an American merchant, has returned from a journey of inspection, and reports his
                    having found large beds of coal near the sea-coast, on the Ta-tong River, west
                    coast of Corea. Several loads of the coal have arrived in junks at Chemulpo, and
                    there the coal was tested on board the U. S. S. Alert, proving to be of better
                    quality than that of Japan, although that tested was but surface scrapings. A
                    second coal-bed has been discovered some 30 miles south of Chemulpo, on the
                    coast.</p>
                <p>Steps are being taken to develop both these beds, an American to work the first
                    and a Chinese company the second.</p>
                <p>I am, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer><signed>GEORGE C. FOULK,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Ensign, U. S. Navy,
                            Chargé d’Affaires ad interim.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
